1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,877
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,158 --> 00:00:06,440
This thirty minute course explains Net Mamba
Plus, the experiment that actually ran,

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,269
and what you can responsibly tell a customer.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:14,744
We will follow the data into training and
inference, inspect real results, and work

5
00:00:14,744 --> 00:00:16,997
through one actual prediction error.

6
00:00:17,278 --> 00:00:20,445
Short practice pauses are included in the
running time.

7
00:00:20,726 --> 00:00:24,835
At the end, you will have ninety seconds to
explain the project aloud.

8
00:00:25,116 --> 00:00:28,133
The narration uses a local synthetic voice.

9
00:00:28,414 --> 00:00:33,400
The experiment records, not the voice or the AI
discussion, are the evidence.

10
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:34,835
Start with three words.

11
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:37,622
A packet is one small network message.

12
00:00:37,903 --> 00:00:41,266
A flow groups related packets into a
conversation.

13
00:00:41,547 --> 00:00:47,369
An intrusion detection system, or I D S,
observes traffic and raises alerts for

14
00:00:47,369 --> 00:00:48,400
investigation.

15
00:00:48,681 --> 00:00:50,887
A classifier assigns categories.

16
00:00:51,169 --> 00:00:56,751
It does not automatically supply packet capture,
connection grouping, an alert policy,

17
00:00:56,751 --> 00:00:57,530
or blocking.

18
00:00:57,811 --> 00:01:03,797
A G P U accelerates numerical operations; it
does not fill in these missing system

19
00:01:03,797 --> 00:01:04,600
components.

20
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:10,668
The paper combines three views of the same flow:
byte content, packet sizes, and time

21
00:01:10,668 --> 00:01:11,025
gaps.

22
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:14,592
Combining these views is what multimodal means
here.

23
00:01:14,872 --> 00:01:18,778
Mamba processes a sequence by updating a
numerical state.

24
00:01:19,058 --> 00:01:20,531
It is not a chatbot.

25
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:23,799
The earlier Net Mamba model used bytes alone.

26
00:01:24,079 --> 00:01:30,009
Our measured work exercises the multimodal Net
Mamba Plus classifier on a G B ten

27
00:01:30,009 --> 00:01:31,400
graphics processor.

28
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:36,476
If the classifier runs successfully, have we
demonstrated a complete live intrusion

29
00:01:36,476 --> 00:01:37,515
detection system?

30
00:01:37,796 --> 00:01:39,100
Take eight seconds.

31
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:47,100
[Practice pause: 8 seconds] Does a working
classifier establish a complete live IDS?

32
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:47,806
No.

33
00:01:48,086 --> 00:01:51,486
Classifier execution is one tested component.

34
00:01:51,766 --> 00:01:56,534
The capture to alert system still needs
implementation and validation.

35
00:01:56,814 --> 00:02:00,000
Keep that boundary visible throughout the
presentation.

36
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,598
The three supplied files have different roles.

37
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,371
The P D F is the research paper.

38
00:02:05,653 --> 00:02:11,235
It describes methods and reports results; it is
not itself a ready to train dataset.

39
00:02:11,517 --> 00:02:21,716
The C I C I D S twenty seventeen C S V contains
1,410,255 packet rows and fifteen

40
00:02:21,716 --> 00:02:22,597
labels.

41
00:02:22,879 --> 00:02:30,909
The U N S W C S V contains 79,881 packet rows
and ten labels.

42
00:02:31,191 --> 00:02:36,667
A C S V is a text table with rows and columns,
not a running database server.

43
00:02:36,948 --> 00:02:41,200
We inspected the supplied exports and recorded
their file identities.

44
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:46,633
Both exports have fifteen hundred payload byte
columns, plus five metadata fields.

45
00:02:46,914 --> 00:02:50,350
T T L relates to a packet’s remaining hop
allowance.

46
00:02:50,632 --> 00:02:53,488
Total length describes recorded packet length.

47
00:02:53,769 --> 00:02:56,217
Protocol identifies a protocol.

48
00:02:56,499 --> 00:02:58,732
Time delta records a time difference.

49
00:02:59,014 --> 00:03:01,762
Label supplies the known example category.

50
00:03:02,044 --> 00:03:07,369
These fields do not establish which connection
each packet belongs to, its direction,

51
00:03:07,369 --> 00:03:09,624
or its order within that connection.

52
00:03:09,906 --> 00:03:15,843
The C I C I D S scan found negative time
differences, and the timing units and

53
00:03:15,843 --> 00:03:19,268
per-flow interpretation remain unestablished.

54
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:25,298
We profiled metadata; we did not train a packet
classifier or numerically validate

55
00:03:25,298 --> 00:03:26,700
every payload value.

56
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:31,787
Imagine a spreadsheet whose next five rows come
from three different conversations.

57
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:36,933
Stacking those rows changes the example into
something that never occurred on the

58
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:37,413
network.

59
00:03:37,695 --> 00:03:42,696
Five neighboring rows are therefore not evidence
of one five-packet flow.

60
00:03:42,978 --> 00:03:46,455
Payload fields also cannot reconstruct missing
headers.

61
00:03:46,737 --> 00:03:51,580
A packet classifier trained directly on these
exports could be useful, but it would be

62
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:52,876
a different experiment.

63
00:03:53,157 --> 00:03:59,109
Reproducing the native input representation
requires established grouping, direction,

64
00:03:59,109 --> 00:04:01,700
ordering, byte extraction and timing.

65
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:07,264
Instead, we acquired the authors’ processed C I
C I O T twenty twenty-two flow

66
00:04:07,264 --> 00:04:07,835
release.

67
00:04:08,117 --> 00:04:12,707
This is a different dataset from C I C I D S
twenty seventeen.

68
00:04:12,989 --> 00:04:21,826
It contains 8,323 training flows, 1,040
validation flows, and 1,041 test flows.

69
00:04:22,108 --> 00:04:24,618
Training examples teach the model’s weights.

70
00:04:24,899 --> 00:04:27,817
Validation selects a candidate checkpoint.

71
00:04:28,098 --> 00:04:31,187
The test split measures that selected
classifier.

72
00:04:31,469 --> 00:04:37,117
The six categories include Flood and R T S P
Brute Force, plus four categories

73
00:04:37,117 --> 00:04:41,100
describing Internet of Things device or power
activity.

74
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:44,851
The native files are J S O N arrays of flow
records.

75
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:50,166
In this release, data contains packet-byte
strings; sizes and intervals are

76
00:04:50,166 --> 00:04:52,314
space-separated numeric strings.

77
00:04:52,595 --> 00:04:55,639
The loader parses them into numerical tensors.

78
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,715
Metadata defines which category each class index
means.

79
00:04:59,996 --> 00:05:05,089
Labels are known answers for training or
evaluation, not legitimate prediction

80
00:05:05,089 --> 00:05:05,676
features.

81
00:05:05,958 --> 00:05:09,902
The code checks types, ranges and consistency
before launch.

82
00:05:10,183 --> 00:05:15,345
A validated stored record still does not prove
that an upstream packet extractor

83
00:05:15,345 --> 00:05:16,700
created it correctly.

84
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:22,449
A colleague gives you five adjacent rows from
one uploaded C S V and asks you to

85
00:05:22,449 --> 00:05:24,174
classify them as a flow.

86
00:05:24,457 --> 00:05:25,599
Would you proceed?

87
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:29,500
Use the next twelve seconds to identify the
missing facts.

88
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:41,500
[Practice pause: 12 seconds] What establishes
relatedness, direction, ordering and extraction?

89
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,758
Do not assume those rows form a flow.

90
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:49,073
Use the compatible released records, or
establish and validate an extractor.

91
00:05:49,355 --> 00:05:54,710
This is the connection between the paper and the
two uploaded tables: they address

92
00:05:54,710 --> 00:06:00,000
related traffic analysis, but their units of
observation are not interchangeable.

93
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,701
Now follow one valid flow through the original
loader.

94
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,967
The byte view keeps up to five packets, with
three hundred twenty stored bytes per

95
00:06:08,967 --> 00:06:09,392
packet.

96
00:06:09,691 --> 00:06:11,816
That gives sixteen hundred values.

97
00:06:12,116 --> 00:06:17,833
The paper describes each packet region as eighty
header bytes plus two hundred forty

98
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:18,786
payload bytes.

99
00:06:19,086 --> 00:06:22,215
Released flows already contain this extraction.

100
00:06:22,515 --> 00:06:25,827
Short records are padded; long ones are
truncated.

101
00:06:26,127 --> 00:06:31,742
The measured byte tensor has batch size by one
by one by sixteen hundred as its

102
00:06:31,742 --> 00:06:32,523
dimensions.

103
00:06:32,823 --> 00:06:38,100
The two other views retain the first twenty
sizes and the first twenty time gaps.

104
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:43,821
The byte transformation divides by two hundred
fifty-five, then uses a mean and

105
00:06:43,821 --> 00:06:46,067
standard deviation of one half.

106
00:06:46,366 --> 00:06:52,164
A zero byte therefore becomes minus one, and two
hundred fifty-five becomes plus one.

107
00:06:52,464 --> 00:06:55,705
Sizes are clipped between zero and fifteen
hundred.

108
00:06:56,005 --> 00:07:02,299
A padding size of fifteen hundred and one is
also clipped to fifteen hundred, so that

109
00:07:02,299 --> 00:07:05,409
boundary cannot reliably identify padding.

110
00:07:05,709 --> 00:07:11,690
For a nonnegative gap x, the loader uses one
plus x divided by two plus x.

111
00:07:11,989 --> 00:07:15,436
Zero becomes one half; one becomes two thirds.

112
00:07:15,736 --> 00:07:19,000
Padded infinite gaps map to one inside the
loader.

113
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,888
For classification, groups of four bytes create
four hundred byte tokens.

114
00:07:24,188 --> 00:07:30,338
Add twenty size tokens, twenty interval tokens
and three summary tokens, for four

115
00:07:30,338 --> 00:07:32,844
hundred forty-three input tokens.

116
00:07:33,144 --> 00:07:37,918
A token here is a numerical input unit, not a
word in a chatbot.

117
00:07:38,218 --> 00:07:43,425
Position and modality information identify its
location and type.

118
00:07:43,725 --> 00:07:46,260
Four Mamba blocks process the sequence.

119
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:52,275
Three modality summaries are added, then a
classification layer produces six raw

120
00:07:52,275 --> 00:07:53,846
outputs called logits.

121
00:07:54,146 --> 00:07:58,509
The model’s internal state belongs to this input
computation.

122
00:07:58,808 --> 00:08:02,600
It is not an implemented cache of live network
connections.

123
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:07,669
During pretraining, the model learns to
reconstruct deliberately hidden input

124
00:08:07,669 --> 00:08:08,261
features.

125
00:08:08,561 --> 00:08:14,231
The source preset masks ninety percent of byte
strides and fifteen percent each of

126
00:08:14,231 --> 00:08:15,615
sizes and intervals.

127
00:08:15,914 --> 00:08:20,936
Reconstruction provides a learning signal
without attack-category targets.

128
00:08:21,236 --> 00:08:24,751
Fine-tuning then learns the six labeled
categories.

129
00:08:25,051 --> 00:08:30,187
A loss measures error; an optimizer changes
weights to reduce that error.

130
00:08:30,487 --> 00:08:33,180
An epoch is a pass through the training loader.

131
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:37,794
Our short pretraining check completed one
hundred thirty-two updates.

132
00:08:38,094 --> 00:08:43,547
The three main fine-tuning runs instead began
from the authors’ released pretrained

133
00:08:43,547 --> 00:08:46,700
checkpoint, not from our short-check checkpoint.

134
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:50,059
After training, inference freezes the learned
weights.

135
00:08:50,359 --> 00:08:54,884
It uses flow features and a selected checkpoint
to produce logits.

136
00:08:55,184 --> 00:09:01,529
Softmax turns the logits into normalized display
scores; the largest output selects a

137
00:09:01,529 --> 00:09:02,201
category.

138
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,951
A checkpoint-bound mapping supplies the category
names.

139
00:09:06,251 --> 00:09:09,999
Without known labels, prediction cannot measure
accuracy.

140
00:09:10,299 --> 00:09:15,510
With labels, evaluation compares the predictions
against those known answers.

141
00:09:15,810 --> 00:09:20,979
Validation chooses the checkpoint; test labels
must not be used to choose a

142
00:09:20,979 --> 00:09:24,700
better-looking model or to supply prediction
features.

143
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,608
Trace the lifecycle aloud.

144
00:09:26,910 --> 00:09:32,120
When do weights change, when is a checkpoint
selected, and when are known test labels

145
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:32,427
used?

146
00:09:32,729 --> 00:09:34,200
Take twelve seconds.

147
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:46,200
[Practice pause: 12 seconds] Train → select
using validation → freeze → test or predict

148
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,981
Training updates the weights.

149
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:50,677
Validation selects the candidate.

150
00:09:50,977 --> 00:09:52,506
Inference freezes it.

151
00:09:52,806 --> 00:09:55,819
Test labels are used afterward for measurement.

152
00:09:56,119 --> 00:10:00,000
This separation is central to a defensible
experiment.

153
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,768
The authors’ repository provides the neural
model, tensor loader and training engines.

154
00:10:06,058 --> 00:10:10,720
Our repository fetches a pinned commit and
verifies source identity.

155
00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:13,176
The model and loader stay unchanged.

156
00:10:13,466 --> 00:10:19,817
For G B ten and C U D A thirteen, separate
native-extension build copies receive

157
00:10:19,817 --> 00:10:24,183
recorded compiler and architecture compatibility
edits.

158
00:10:24,473 --> 00:10:29,600
This is a compatibility port, not proof of an
identical paper environment.

159
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,425
Think of the wrapper as the lab’s coordinator.

160
00:10:32,714 --> 00:10:38,501
The reproduction launcher validates the source
and inputs, resolves settings, starts

161
00:10:38,501 --> 00:10:42,359
the original training program, and saves a run
manifest.

162
00:10:42,648 --> 00:10:48,390
The evaluation entry point strictly reloads a
classifier and scores the native test

163
00:10:48,390 --> 00:10:48,806
split.

164
00:10:49,095 --> 00:10:54,237
The prediction entry point handles already
assembled flows without known labels.

165
00:10:54,527 --> 00:11:00,282
The replay entry point records outputs,
independently recomputes metrics, and creates

166
00:11:00,282 --> 00:11:01,501
a browser display.

167
00:11:01,790 --> 00:11:06,800
These tools share preparation checks while
keeping their execution jobs distinct.

168
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:12,141
Configuration files describe the source pin,
assets and experiment recipe.

169
00:11:12,431 --> 00:11:15,079
Requirements describe parts of the runtime.

170
00:11:15,369 --> 00:11:18,723
Tools build, measure, review and publish
outputs.

171
00:11:19,013 --> 00:11:22,500
Tests check important acceptance and failure
behavior.

172
00:11:22,789 --> 00:11:27,954
The evidence folder holds actual manifests,
logs, predictions and hashes.

173
00:11:28,243 --> 00:11:32,481
Customer documents translate those records into
a presentation.

174
00:11:32,771 --> 00:11:38,830
Research documents preserve curated findings and
council decisions; they are not a raw

175
00:11:38,830 --> 00:11:40,098
private chat dump.

176
00:11:40,387 --> 00:11:45,638
The complete file walkthrough explains every
tracked file and download, so you can

177
00:11:45,638 --> 00:11:48,200
find the exact record behind a question.

178
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:53,900
A manifest records what was requested, which
source and data were used, the checkpoint

179
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:56,948
identity, class mapping and completion status.

180
00:11:57,237 --> 00:12:00,256
A hash is a fingerprint of exact file bytes.

181
00:12:00,546 --> 00:12:05,856
It helps detect drift; it does not prove
scientific correctness or usage rights.

182
00:12:06,146 --> 00:12:10,332
Keep a checkpoint with its successful manifest
or export provenance.

183
00:12:10,622 --> 00:12:16,049
Choose a fresh output directory each time so new
work does not overwrite earlier

184
00:12:16,049 --> 00:12:16,659
evidence.

185
00:12:16,949 --> 00:12:22,700
Raw data, downloaded source and trained weights
remain separate local research assets.

186
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:27,854
Which description should you use: we replaced
the authors’ model, or we wrapped pinned

187
00:12:27,854 --> 00:12:30,431
original code and ported its native builds?

188
00:12:30,721 --> 00:12:34,100
Take ten seconds and name the wrapper’s main
additions.

189
00:12:34,100 --> 00:12:44,100
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Name the original
pieces and the added checks.

190
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:46,067
The second description is correct.

191
00:12:46,358 --> 00:12:51,516
We added a checked execution route, inference
tools, measurements, provenance and

192
00:12:51,516 --> 00:12:54,382
teaching materials around the original model.

193
00:12:54,672 --> 00:13:00,000
Compiler compatibility changes do not establish
exact paper reproduction.

194
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:04,261
Now separate things that were executed from
things that were proposed.

195
00:13:04,544 --> 00:13:12,837
All three declared fine-tuning seeds completed
120 epochs and 7,920 optimizer updates

196
00:13:12,837 --> 00:13:13,325
each.

197
00:13:13,609 --> 00:13:18,670
A seed controls random choices, such as
initialization and sample order.

198
00:13:18,954 --> 00:13:24,209
These were three runs on the same dataset split,
not three independent datasets.

199
00:13:24,493 --> 00:13:27,975
Validation accuracy selected each best
checkpoint.

200
00:13:28,259 --> 00:13:32,476
The selected classifier was then reloaded for
test evaluation.

201
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:38,291
Logs and optimizer records support the completed
budgets; the recipe alone would not

202
00:13:38,291 --> 00:13:40,200
prove that the work finished.

203
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:50,924
The test accuracies were 91.26% for seed zero,
84.05% for seed one, and 84.63% for

204
00:13:50,924 --> 00:13:52,101
seed two.

205
00:13:52,385 --> 00:13:55,420
Their mean was 86.65%.

206
00:13:55,704 --> 00:13:59,411
We retained every seed rather than hiding the
weaker runs.

207
00:13:59,696 --> 00:14:05,326
Seed zero was chosen for the demonstration in
advance, not selected afterward because

208
00:14:05,326 --> 00:14:06,784
it had the best score.

209
00:14:07,068 --> 00:14:12,061
You can show that run to make the demo concrete,
but you should report the three-run

210
00:14:12,061 --> 00:14:14,200
mean when describing the experiment.

211
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:18,012
Accuracy is the fraction of examples classified
correctly.

212
00:14:18,296 --> 00:14:23,901
Precision asks how often a predicted category is
right; recall asks how many true

213
00:14:23,901 --> 00:14:26,461
examples of that category were found.

214
00:14:26,746 --> 00:14:29,106
F one balances precision and recall.

215
00:14:29,391 --> 00:14:34,588
Macro F one gives classes equal weight; weighted
F one weights their support.

216
00:14:34,873 --> 00:14:40,356
A confusion matrix shows true categories against
predicted categories so aggregate

217
00:14:40,356 --> 00:14:42,496
scores do not hide the mistakes.

218
00:14:42,781 --> 00:14:49,278
The sample standard deviation across accuracy
results was 4.00 percentage points.

219
00:14:49,562 --> 00:14:55,269
It is a descriptive spread, not a confidence
interval and not a promise about customer

220
00:14:55,269 --> 00:14:55,800
traffic.

221
00:14:55,800 --> 00:15:02,385
The paper reports 97.50% accuracy for this
benchmark under different conditions.

222
00:15:02,669 --> 00:15:04,640
We did not reproduce that result.

223
00:15:04,924 --> 00:15:10,858
Source-based fine-tuning used batch size one
hundred twenty-eight and an effective

224
00:15:10,858 --> 00:15:14,693
learning rate of zero point zero zero one on G B
ten.

225
00:15:14,977 --> 00:15:20,879
The paper describes batch sixty-four, rate zero
point zero zero two, and A one hundred

226
00:15:20,879 --> 00:15:21,497
hardware.

227
00:15:21,781 --> 00:15:25,680
The full pretraining history of the released
checkpoint is unknown.

228
00:15:25,964 --> 00:15:30,795
We did not repeat the paper’s full one hundred
fifty thousand step pretraining.

229
00:15:31,079 --> 00:15:36,022
The cause of the accuracy gap has not been
isolated; it would be speculation to

230
00:15:36,022 --> 00:15:38,400
attribute all of it to one difference.

231
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:43,919
The input audit found five raw-input overlaps
between training and validation and six

232
00:15:43,919 --> 00:15:45,607
between training and test.

233
00:15:45,891 --> 00:15:50,807
Physical independence and the released
checkpoint’s previous exposure remain

234
00:15:50,807 --> 00:15:51,713
unestablished.

235
00:15:51,997 --> 00:15:54,485
These findings belong beside the metrics.

236
00:15:54,769 --> 00:16:00,834
Repeating an evaluation on the same test data
checks repeatability; it does not create

237
00:16:00,834 --> 00:16:02,950
an independent validation set.

238
00:16:03,234 --> 00:16:08,905
Independent customer traffic, with reviewed
incident labels, is still needed before

239
00:16:08,905 --> 00:16:12,800
operational accuracy or false-alert rates can be
claimed.

240
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:15,481
Choose the sentence you would say to the
customer.

241
00:16:15,765 --> 00:16:19,874
We reproduced the paper’s ninety-seven point
five percent result.

242
00:16:20,158 --> 00:16:27,381
Or, three runs averaged 86.65% on the released
split, and exact paper reproduction

243
00:16:27,381 --> 00:16:28,526
remains open.

244
00:16:28,810 --> 00:16:33,200
Take twelve seconds to include one reason the
comparison has limits.

245
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:45,200
[Practice pause: 12 seconds] State the measured
result and one comparison limitation.

246
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:47,499
The second sentence is defensible.

247
00:16:47,784 --> 00:16:53,241
Keep the runtime, data split, source settings
and unknown pretraining history with the

248
00:16:53,241 --> 00:16:53,685
result.

249
00:16:53,969 --> 00:16:59,598
Avoid turning the best seed, a spread statistic,
or repeated testing into a stronger

250
00:16:59,598 --> 00:17:00,000
claim.

251
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:06,512
The working browser demonstration displays
predictions recorded from a real seed zero

252
00:17:06,512 --> 00:17:08,045
G P U inference run.

253
00:17:08,347 --> 00:17:14,081
Across one thousand forty-one labeled test
flows, nine hundred fifty predictions were

254
00:17:14,081 --> 00:17:16,644
correct and ninety-one were incorrect.

255
00:17:16,946 --> 00:17:21,198
The page can replay these records, filter them
and show mistakes.

256
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:27,046
Opening it does not run the neural model again,
inspect your computer’s traffic, or

257
00:17:27,046 --> 00:17:27,982
block packets.

258
00:17:28,283 --> 00:17:31,662
Its speed control changes the pace of the
display.

259
00:17:31,964 --> 00:17:36,400
It is unrelated to model inference latency or
network throughput.

260
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:42,987
The release’s class mapping contains Flood, R T
S P Brute Force, Audio, Other, Cameras

261
00:17:42,987 --> 00:17:46,586
and Home Automation under their recorded names.

262
00:17:46,888 --> 00:17:50,429
The last four describe device or power activity.

263
00:17:50,731 --> 00:17:55,698
This is not a universal benign, malicious, and
unknown-attack system.

264
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,714
The replay has four hundred two predictions in
the two attack categories.

265
00:18:01,015 --> 00:18:05,476
That does not mean four hundred two verified
operational alerts.

266
00:18:05,778 --> 00:18:12,104
An alert policy must account for traffic
context, uncertainty, unknown activity and

267
00:18:12,104 --> 00:18:14,010
the cost of false alarms.

268
00:18:14,312 --> 00:18:17,600
None of that can be inferred merely from a class
label.

269
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:21,906
Consider the actual saved row numbered six
hundred thirty-five.

270
00:18:22,209 --> 00:18:30,378
Its highest-scoring category is class one, R T S
P Brute Force, with a display score

271
00:18:30,378 --> 00:18:31,351
of 84.43%.

272
00:18:31,653 --> 00:18:34,417
The other five scores are visible beside it.

273
00:18:34,719 --> 00:18:37,621
First identify what the model predicted.

274
00:18:37,923 --> 00:18:41,677
Then distinguish that prediction from what
actually happened.

275
00:18:41,979 --> 00:18:48,247
The six scores sum to one after softmax, but
this normalization does not establish

276
00:18:48,247 --> 00:18:49,164
calibration.

277
00:18:49,466 --> 00:18:54,636
A high score says the model favors one of these
classes; it does not prove an

278
00:18:54,636 --> 00:18:59,000
eighty-four percent chance that a customer
experienced an attack.

279
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,727
Now reveal the known dataset label: class three,
Other.

280
00:19:03,029 --> 00:19:08,435
The prediction disagrees with that label, so
this is a recorded classification error.

281
00:19:08,737 --> 00:19:14,530
We deliberately selected it to examine a
mistake, not to estimate how often such

282
00:19:14,530 --> 00:19:15,616
mistakes occur.

283
00:19:15,918 --> 00:19:20,174
One row cannot establish an operational
false-positive rate.

284
00:19:20,476 --> 00:19:25,815
The example is useful because the score looked
confident even though the labeled

285
00:19:25,815 --> 00:19:27,216
answer was different.

286
00:19:27,518 --> 00:19:33,490
In a real deployment, an analyst would need
traffic context and a validated alert

287
00:19:33,490 --> 00:19:34,006
policy.

288
00:19:34,308 --> 00:19:39,300
Neither the classifier nor this video verifies
that an attack occurred.

289
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:45,368
Use the next eighteen seconds to explain this
row to a customer in three sentences.

290
00:19:45,670 --> 00:19:51,273
Say what the model predicted, name the known
dataset label, and state what the score

291
00:19:51,273 --> 00:19:52,407
cannot establish.

292
00:19:52,710 --> 00:19:58,193
Do not call the score a calibrated attack
probability, and do not extrapolate a

293
00:19:58,193 --> 00:20:00,900
false-alert rate from this one example.

294
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:18,900
[Practice pause: 18 seconds] Explain the
predicted class, known label and score
limitation.

295
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:25,058
A good explanation is: the model predicted R T S
P Brute Force, but the dataset

296
00:20:25,058 --> 00:20:27,163
labeled this flow as Other.

297
00:20:27,465 --> 00:20:33,933
The 84.43% display score is not an established
probability of attack.

298
00:20:34,235 --> 00:20:40,583
This recorded mistake shows why operational use
needs independent validation and an

299
00:20:40,583 --> 00:20:41,578
alert policy.

300
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:46,834
You can reproduce the display from the saved
prediction file without rerunning the

301
00:20:46,834 --> 00:20:47,197
model.

302
00:20:47,499 --> 00:20:53,253
For fresh predictions, use the separate G P U
route and a provenance-bound checkpoint.

303
00:20:53,555 --> 00:20:59,446
Keep recorded replay, new model execution and
live traffic monitoring distinct in the

304
00:20:59,446 --> 00:21:00,000
meeting.

305
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:05,028
You do not need to install the research stack
just to understand or present this work.

306
00:21:05,309 --> 00:21:10,573
The browser course, recorded replay, P D F and
PowerPoint are the viewing route.

307
00:21:10,853 --> 00:21:16,790
A second route uses Python three point ten or
newer to check the harness and published

308
00:21:16,790 --> 00:21:18,171
evidence on a C P U.

309
00:21:18,452 --> 00:21:23,758
The third route builds the tested G B ten
environment for new model execution.

310
00:21:24,039 --> 00:21:30,083
Keeping these routes separate saves time and
prevents a common mistake: thinking that

311
00:21:30,083 --> 00:21:35,700
opening a presentation or passing a C P U test
has rerun the neural experiment.

312
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:41,265
Clone the repository, enter the project folder
and run the unit-test command shown on

313
00:21:41,265 --> 00:21:41,723
screen.

314
00:21:42,005 --> 00:21:45,049
Then run the package verifier and course
verifier.

315
00:21:45,330 --> 00:21:50,105
The test suite should finish with O K, and the
verifiers should report passed for

316
00:21:50,105 --> 00:21:51,697
their deterministic checks.

317
00:21:51,978 --> 00:21:55,300
The audited release recorded fifty-four harness
tests.

318
00:21:55,582 --> 00:22:00,469
The interactive course added seventeen more,
bringing that snapshot to seventy-one.

319
00:22:00,750 --> 00:22:03,687
Video verification is recorded separately.

320
00:22:03,969 --> 00:22:09,250
These are checks of the code and delivered
artifacts; they do not download new

321
00:22:09,250 --> 00:22:12,500
training data or repeat the G P U training runs.

322
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:17,759
The package verifier recomputes metrics from
saved predictions, compares model

323
00:22:17,759 --> 00:22:23,289
identities, checks hashes, and verifies that
presentation material agrees with its

324
00:22:23,289 --> 00:22:23,761
source.

325
00:22:24,041 --> 00:22:29,179
Two separately built native environments also
passed seven numerical checks each.

326
00:22:29,460 --> 00:22:33,464
Strict saved-model evaluations agreed for all
three seeds.

327
00:22:33,745 --> 00:22:39,387
A full seed zero unlabeled recheck matched all
one thousand forty-one predicted

328
00:22:39,387 --> 00:22:41,601
classes and logits in that run.

329
00:22:41,882 --> 00:22:46,870
These results support execution and
repeatability under recorded conditions.

330
00:22:47,151 --> 00:22:51,433
If a file or checksum differs, preserve the
failure and investigate.

331
00:22:51,714 --> 00:22:56,771
Blindly regenerating checksums would only
replace the fingerprint; it would not

332
00:22:56,771 --> 00:22:58,500
